{# Editorial diagrams, generated as standalone SVG images by
   scripts/generate-explanatory-figures.mjs. Referenced through a plain <img>, each
   one scales as a single opaque image (site default: img{max-width:100%;height:auto})
   rather than as part of the page's live DOM and CSS. #}

{% macro moneyVersusProof() %}
<figure class="article-figure">
<img src="/assets/images/posts/ac-033-money-versus-proof.svg" width="1200" height="1530"
     alt="A grid plotting five options by proof, left to right, against money, bottom to top. Fix the process sits at money 4, proof 4. Buy a CRM add-on sits at money 4, proof 3. Automate with AI sits at money 5, proof 3. Build custom sits alone in the shaded top-left corner at money 5, proof 2: the highest money score with the weakest proof. Do nothing sits at money 0, proof 5.">
<figcaption>The worked example from step 5, plotted. The shaded corner is the pair to watch: a big number nobody has checked.</figcaption>
</figure>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro decisionPath() %}
<figure class="article-figure">
<img src="/assets/images/posts/ac-033-decision-path.svg" width="1200" height="1634"
     alt="Eight steps in order: ask where the business loses money; pull the real numbers; sort them into facts, fair guesses and unknowns; write down all five options; score them; choose and write down why; run one ninety-day test with a stop rule; ask six questions before approving spend. A callout after the choosing step reads: if nothing clears the bar, stop and say so out loud.">
<figcaption>Two hours of work, and a real exit. Most of the method happens before anyone writes a line of code.</figcaption>
</figure>
{% endmacro %}
